{"id":"rdb-1ed603","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"`sqlLiteral` in `engine/engine.go` escapes single quotes but does not escape backslash characters when interpolating string parameters into SQL.\n\nThe underlying go-mysql-server (Vitess-derived) SQL parser treats `\\` as an escape character inside string literals. Any backslash in a string value is silently consumed during parsing, corrupting the stored data.\n\n## Impact\n\nAffects any string column containing backslashes, especially **JSON data** where backslash escapes are pervasive (`\\\"`, `\\\\`, `\\n`, `\\t`, etc.). A JSON object stored via a parameterized query comes back with its escape sequences mangled.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```go\nctx := context.Background()\neng := engine.New()\ns := eng.NewSession(ctx)\ns.Exec(ctx, \"CREATE TABLE t (data TEXT)\")\ns.Exec(ctx, \"INSERT INTO t (data) VALUES (?)\", `{\"msg\":\"hello \\\"world\\\"\"}`)\nresult, _ := s.Query(ctx, \"SELECT data FROM t\")\n// Expected: {\"msg\":\"hello \\\"world\\\"\"}\n// Actual:   {\"msg\":\"hello \"world\"\"}   — backslashes eaten, JSON is now invalid\n```\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nIn `sqlLiteral`, escape backslashes before single quotes:\n\n```go\ncase string:\n    s := strings.ReplaceAll(value, `\\`, `\\\\`)\n    s = strings.ReplaceAll(s, \"'\", \"''\")\n    return \"'\" + s + \"'\", nil\n```\n\nMigrated from GitHub issue nicbet/repodb#1.","labels":["bug"],"sort_order":"a0","status":"PLANNED","title":"sqlLiteral does not escape backslashes, corrupting JSON round-trips"},"created_at":"2026-09-14T06:06:37.243946Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.270 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-cf0385","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Currently there is no support for `ALTER TABLE` statements. When a consumer (e.g. nate) discovers new columns or needs to change column types during a migration, the only option is to drop and recreate the entire table — which causes data loss.\n\n## Desired behavior\n\nSupport at minimum:\n- `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN`\n- `ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN`\n- `ALTER TABLE ... MODIFY COLUMN` / `ALTER COLUMN ... SET DATA TYPE`\n- `ALTER TABLE ... RENAME COLUMN`\n\nThis would allow consumers to evolve their schemas incrementally without destroying existing data.\n\n## Motivation\n\nnate (agent trace recorder) frequently needs to adjust its schema as new event kinds or metadata fields are added. Today each migration is destructive — the table must be dropped and recreated, losing all historical trace data. With ALTER TABLE support, migrations become additive and non-destructive.","labels":["feature"],"sort_order":"a0","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Support ALTER TABLE for schema migrations"},"created_at":"2026-09-14T06:06:42.193615Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.270 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
